On Mon, 15 Dec 2008, Ray Dillinger wrote:
> David Ploog wrote:
>
>> To be brutally honest, as a developer of another product on the market (I
>> don't think in these terms, actually): Crawl benefits from Nethack's
>> development being stalled.  Other roguelikes do too, of course. Nethack
>> has shortcomings whose non-fixing will help other games, including Crawl.
>> So yes, you lose players.
>
> I adjusted the punctuation there... I think the above is what you meant?

Yes, thank you.

> To me Nethack is a case study in game design.  It's very good.  It is
> in fact the defining example of its niche in roguelike games.  In the
> long run though, it wasn't the first roguelike game and it won't be the
> last.  It is, however, the roguelike game from which I've learned the
> most about the design of roguelike games and the one I most admire.

Since it is the only other roguelike I played, I learned a huge amount 
about design from Nethack. I cannot say I admire it anymore, though: the 
design seems too erratic in many places. For example, lots of little 
details added in the game are made useless by other features. I don't 
think that's good. In order to be concrete, some examples:
* Unihorns taking care of most ailments and making several potions 
and spells useless (except for polyfodder). Yes, a spell like Cure 
Sickness has its niche, but it is really small.
* HP gain per XL has been carefully balanced using Con. This is rendered 
moot when alchemy comes into play.
* AC is an interesting invariant of characters, until they reach very low 
numbers, making all gear irrelevant. By this time, combat is made 
senseless, too.

To me, it seems as if the devteam consisted of powergamers and balancers, 
the former more often getting their say than the latter. (See the rather 
late addition of the potion of Full Healing, for example.)

> I'm also something of a roguelike developer; my game is a lot more
> vaporous than Crawl as of yet, so you probably have more insight here.
> But I credit nethack (the game) as a positive inspiration, and nethack
> (the codebase) as a negative or cautionary one.

Cannot say anything about the code. But I know that Crawl was written 
because the original authour wanted to avoid some deficits of Angband and 
Nethack. (In Nethack, some of these are: permanent resistances from 
eating; identical ascension kits; broken features like Elbereth and 
genocide.)

> And that, sort of, has been my inspiration and idea as I've worked
> on my game.  I try very hard to make the fundamental game "engine"
> general enough to support _easily_ a game as complex as nethack, and
> with a generally robust framework to support complexities that no
> one has yet considered.

Nethack certainly has strengths: the starting pet (and their behaviour) 
is one, the shops are another. When I was a Nethack player, I thought that 
the extreme care about minute details (TDTTOE) is awesome, but I am not 
convinced anymore. In some sense, this approach paved the way to the 
current "spoiler & win" status and it also killed a part of the game 
inside (for me).

> Perhaps I am chasing rainbows.  Perhaps it will never be finished. 
> Sometimes I work on it for months, and sometimes I get tired of it and 
> put it aside for a year and a half.

Good luck! Are you working on a grand epic, or could you release a smaller 
but playable version?

>> Something more concrete: Nethack is behind the times when it comes to the
>> interface. I've lost count on how often I've read these lines on r.g.r.n:
>>   newbie: "It would sure help if they'd make hp losses more visible."
>>   oldbie: "It is the player's task to watch hp all the time."
>> This is backwards design and just one example.
>
> Right.  You can always work on the UI. Especially in a game with
> dozens of modes and hundreds of one-character commands. For my
> money the biggest barrier to new users is learning all the commands
> and what they do.  They're used to games where they have maybe six
> or eight buttons that do things; literally hundreds of commands is
> not something they're used to having to keep in their heads, and that
> doesn't even begin to let them know which ones are good and bad
> ideas in which situation.

I think the same. (Crawl's has an awful amount of commands, too.) Of 
course, some of the later changes (introduction of the #advanced commands) 
and hearsay of #tip seems to go in the wrong direction for my tastes.

If you are interested, I reckon that any modern roguelike needs to come 
with Tiles (alongside to ASCII) and with a tutorial. It is good to keep 
server play in mind (even if you don't care about in your first released 
version, the architecture should allow adding it later).

>> As far as I understood, the best players can win Nethack in a rather 
>> unconditional way. This means that the learning curve is done with.
>
> True.  There really are people for whom the above statement [everyone
> is still in the learning curve] is false.  I consider them to be more
> the exception than the rule, however.  Most players are still on the
> learning curve.

I see. This makes sense, yes. I think that a game should challenge its 
master players, too, but that's a different design principle.

> I think that this was an important design goal of the game; in
> principle nearly all deaths are avoidable.

I really hope this was the case.

> But just making it unsolved wouldn't fix the problem.  If I tinkered
> the code so truly unavoidable deaths happened with some percentage
> chance, it would not make the game better.  You still couldn't compare
> the best players.  They would win when they didn't get one and lose
> when they did.  In order to make it possible to compare players, you
> have to have chances of loss that are avoidable in some way. Only
> then may the best distinguish themselves by surviving them more often
> than others.  And that is the design principle on which nethack is
> founded.

Yes, you would end up with a completely different game. Crawl approaches 
this by putting much more emphasis on variance (randomness). Little is 
guaranteed -- you can find dungeons without resistances, or with 
horrendous OOD monsters. Needless to say, this can be lethal (the former 
in a strategical sense, the latter quite direct). Good players are those 
which can utilise the resources thrown at them to overcomes these 
obstacles. (Crawl has seen very impressive streaks, as well. Too long for 
my tastes.)

> That said, I'm not sure I agree with that design principle.  Although
> I think *most* games should be winnable, I don't consider it necessary
> in a roguelike that *every* game be winnable.

That's what I think, too. However, I definitely respect the principle of 
"a started game is winnable and only lost by player mistakes". Its appeal, 
as well as its drawback, is the proximity to puzzle/patience games. Also, 
we (roguelike developers) are not able to construct really good challenges 
for the player (a la, "the player has cold resistance and this set of 
tools available, by using Foo and Bar he could overcome that group of 
monsters"), which a human GM could. This is why I am afraid that in 
reality the Nethack principle leads to tedium when someone tries to go for 
the 100% win rates.

>> I am as curious as everyone else, and I sure would love to know about the
>> devteam's opinion on these topics. (In particular, if winnability from the
>> start was a set design goal, or an accident.) However, we will never know,
>> and the reason could simply be that no one cares anymore.
>
> I care.  I care passionately about such issues of game design.

Oh, I knew that someone would care. :) I was talking of devteam members.

> And I tell you this, winnability of every game was very definitely a 
> goal in the design of nethack; the game is balanced on this principle 
> like a needle on the edge of a razor.  It is not even remotely possible
> that it is accidental.

I take your word for it.


> At this time it appears that Spork and Slash'em may be nethack's
> successors-apparent.  And that may be all right, too.  The genre
> evolves; the set of games changes from time to time.

Definitely.

Thanks for the reply, I love to discuss design.
David


